      "id": "draft.pilot7.diversity.01",
      "stem": "An enrolment service runs on Cloud A and Cloud B using different operating-system platforms. A drill makes every Cloud A service unavailable for 30 minutes. The requirement is to resume validated submissions, including new user sign-ins, within five minutes while preserving fresh MFA, authorisation and current revocation checks. Both submission paths currently call the sole identity provider hosted in Cloud A; existing tokens cannot satisfy this workflow without those fresh checks. Testing confirms that Cloud B has usable data and sufficient capacity, and its network, DNS, keys and failover controls require no Cloud A service. An improvement may add at most $5,000 per month; the listed costs are fixed. Which single plan addresses the remaining shared failure dependency while meeting these requirements?",
      "options": [
        "Add a third compute deployment at another cloud for $4,500 per month, keeping the sole identity provider in Cloud A.",
        "Change Cloud B to another operating-system platform for $2,500 per month, keeping the sole identity provider in Cloud A.",
        "Add an approved, pre-provisioned identity path in Cloud B for $4,000 per month; it has protected current identity/policy state, needs no Cloud A service, and passes the required security and submission checks in three minutes with Cloud A unavailable.",
        "Keep one-hour cached tokens for $1,000 per month and bypass fresh MFA and current revocation checks during the Cloud A outage."
      ],
      "correctOption": 2,
      "optionFeedback": [
        "A third compute deployment still needs the sole identity provider in Cloud A. During the stated Cloud A outage, it cannot perform the fresh MFA and revocation checks required for submissions; provider count does not remove that shared dependency.",
        "Changing Cloud B to another operating-system platform does not make the Cloud A identity provider available. The existing operating-system diversity already leaves both submission paths dependent on the same authentication service.",
        "Correct: this approved path operates without Cloud A, preserves required MFA, authorisation and revocation checks, and resumes validated submissions in three minutes. Its $4,000 monthly cost is within the $5,000 cap. The test supports this stated failure case, not universal independence.",
        "Bypassing fresh MFA and current revocation checks violates the explicit security requirements. Keeping old tokens may help other permitted workflows, but it cannot make this submission process acceptable under the stated policy."
      ],
      "explanation": "Correct: this approved path operates without Cloud A, preserves required MFA, authorisation and revocation checks, and resumes validated submissions in three minutes. Its $4,000 monthly cost is within the $5,000 cap. The test supports this stated failure case, not universal independence.",
      "format": "mcq",
      "cognitiveSkill": "scenario_application",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "difficultyStatus": "proposed_uncalibrated",
      "objectiveCodesProposal": [
        "3.4"
      ],
      "objectiveScopeProposal": "platform_diversity_and_multicloud_shared_failure_context",
      "objectiveScopeApproved": false,
      "primaryConceptProposal": null,
      "secondaryConceptProposals": [],
      "taxonomyBlocker": "No approved mastery identity for correlated runtime failures across diverse platforms/clouds is established. Do not promote cloud labels or an RTO/IAM contrast into approved canonical mastery roles.",
      "scenarioFamilyProposal": "diverse-platforms-shared-authentication-fate",
      "familyRelation": "proposed_distinct_reasoning_pending_independent_gate",
      "familyRationale": "Assess the required runtime dependency that survives compute/provider/platform diversity and permits one outage to block both paths. draft.pilot1.bia.02 and its worksheet row prioritise restoring a shared identity resource; draft.pilot1.rto.02 sums one serial restoration path. This item instead rejects added compute or OS diversity that preserves the shared runtime cut point, and selects a tested independent secure path. m451 concerns compromise powers from a shared privileged token, not continuity during identity-provider outage. Related shared-dependency reasoning is disclosed; local editorial distinction remains proposed and may be merged at the independent gate, with no psychometric independence claim.",
